<TeXmacs|2.1.4>

<style|generic>

<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|<ATHENA> - Compiling>>

  <label|H1 Introduction>

  <section|Introduction>

  This document describes how to build <ATHENA> from source.

  The supported build target is x86_64 GNU/Linux with Qt 6 and Wayland. The
  reference development environment is openSUSE Tumbleweed snapshot 20260812,
  KDE Plasma/KWin 6.7.4 on Wayland, and Qt 6.11.1. The Tumbleweed build with
  Intel oneAPI <verbatim|icx>/<verbatim|icpx> is the configuration currently
  expected to compile without source changes.

  Intel oneAPI C++ (<verbatim|icpx>) is the recommended compiler. Install the
  current Intel oneAPI DPC++/C++ Compiler using the instructions on the
  <hlink|Intel compiler download and installation
  page|https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/tools/oneapi/dpc-compiler-download.html>.

  <label|H1 Building on x64 GNU/Linux>

  <section|Building <ATHENA> on x64 GNU/Linux>

  To build <ATHENA> for direct use, follow sections 2.1 through 2.4. To build
  an RPM, DEB, or AppImage, obtain the source as in 2.1 and then use 2.5.

  <label|H2 Obtaining the source>

  <subsection|Obtaining the source>

  <\code>
    git clone https://github.com/NuaptanEvalisk/ATHENA.git

    cd ATHENA
  </code>

  Checkout the branch or tag that you intend to build.

  <label|H2 Installing dependencies>

  <subsection|Installing dependencies>

  Install the corresponding development packages for your distribution. On
  current openSUSE Tumbleweed, the following is an example:

  <\code>
    sudo zypper install \\

    \ \ git cmake ninja pkgconf-pkg-config patchelf \\

    \ \ autoconf automake libtool make makeinfo gperf bison flex \\

    \ \ qt6-base-devel qt6-svg-devel qt6-qt5compat-devel \\

    \ \ qt6-wayland-devel qt6-waylandclient-devel \\

    \ \ kf6-kio-devel kf6-kconfigwidgets-devel \\

    \ \ kf6-kcompletion-devel kf6-syntax-highlighting-devel \\

    \ \ boost-devel mimalloc-devel sqlite3-devel spdlog-devel hunspell-devel
    \\

    \ \ freetype2-devel fontconfig-devel harfbuzz-devel \\

    \ \ libpng16-devel libjpeg8-devel zlib-devel libzstd-devel \\

    \ \ libsodium-devel ImageMagick-devel libgnutls-devel gmp-devel \\

    \ \ libffi-devel libunistring-devel readline-devel ncurses-devel \\

    \ \ libcurl-devel ghostscript resvg-devel \\

    \ \ python3 python313-uv cargo rust
  </code>

  <label|remark:A system Guile installation is neither needed nor used CMake
  builds the vendored Guile 3 runtime aut {>

  <\remark>
    A system Guile installation is neither needed nor used. CMake builds the
    vendored <ATHENA> Guile 3 runtime automatically. Do not configure the
    build against system Guile.
  </remark>

  <label|remark:A system Guile installation is neither needed nor used CMake
  builds the vendored Guile 3 runtime aut }>

  <label|H3 Compiling libtcc>

  <subsubsection*|Compiling libtcc>

  If no usable libtcc development package is available, build TinyCC from
  source:

  <\code>
    git clone https://repo.or.cz/tinycc.git

    cd tinycc

    CC=icx ./configure

    make -j16
  </code>

  Pass the resulting header and library to the <ATHENA> CMake configuration:

  <\code>
    -DTCC_INCLUDE_DIR=/path/to/tinycc \\

    -DTCC_LIBRARY=/path/to/tinycc/libtcc.a
  </code>

  <label|H3 Compiling llama.cpp>

  <subsubsection*|Compiling llama.cpp>

  <ATHENA> currently builds against the llama.cpp revision used by its
  container toolchain: <verbatim|66c4f9ded01b29d9120255be1ed8d5835bcbb51d>. A
  CPU build can be produced with:

  <\code>
    git clone https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp.git

    cd llama.cpp

    git checkout 66c4f9ded01b29d9120255be1ed8d5835bcbb51d

    source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh

    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx \\

    \ \ -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_BUILD_COMMON=ON \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_BUILD_TOOLS=OFF

    cmake --build build -j16
  </code>

  For an Intel SYCL-enabled build, configure llama.cpp with the additional
  backend options:

  <\code>
    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx \\

    \ \ -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON \\

    \ \ -DGGML_SYCL=ON \\

    \ \ -DGGML_SYCL_TARGET=INTEL \\

    \ \ -DGGML_SYCL_DNN=ON \\

    \ \ -DGGML_SYCL_GRAPH=ON \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_BUILD_COMMON=ON \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_BUILD_TOOLS=OFF

    cmake --build build -j16
  </code>

  <label|H2 Compiling ATHENA>

  <subsection|Compiling <ATHENA>>

  Load the oneAPI environment and choose the build parallelism. The
  <verbatim|ATHENA_BUILD_JOBS> variable controls the private runtime builds;
  <verbatim|-j> controls the outer CMake build.

  <\code>
    source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh

    JOBS=16

    export ATHENA_BUILD_JOBS="$JOBS"
  </code>

  For a Debug build in <verbatim|build_qt6>:

  <\code>
    CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build_qt6 -G Ninja \\

    \ \ -DATHENA_GUI=Qt6 \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_CPP_SOURCE_DIR=/path/to/llama.cpp

    cmake --build build_qt6 -j"$JOBS"
  </code>

  For a <verbatim|RelWithDebInfo> build in <verbatim|build_rel>:

  <\code>
    CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build_rel -G Ninja \\

    \ \ -DATHENA_GUI=Qt6 \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_CPP_SOURCE_DIR=/path/to/llama.cpp

    cmake --build build_rel -j"$JOBS"
  </code>

  If libtcc was built in a non-system location, add the
  <verbatim|TCC_INCLUDE_DIR> and <verbatim|TCC_LIBRARY> arguments from 2.2 to
  the CMake configuration.

  <label|remark:The first build may spend a substantial amount of time
  bootstrapping the vendored Guile runtime Thin {>

  <\remark>
    The first build may spend a substantial amount of time bootstrapping the
    vendored Guile runtime. ThinLTO links may also take a long time.
  </remark>

  <label|remark:The first build may spend a substantial amount of time
  bootstrapping the vendored Guile runtime Thin }>

  <label|H3 Compiling ATHENA with ThreadSanitizer enabled>

  <subsubsection*|Compiling <ATHENA> with ThreadSanitizer enabled>

  <\code>
    CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build_tsan -G Ninja \\

    \ \ -DATHENA_GUI=Qt6 \\

    \ \ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \\

    \ \ -DATHENA_ENABLE_TSAN=ON \\

    \ \ -DLLAMA_CPP_SOURCE_DIR=/path/to/llama.cpp

    cmake --build build_tsan -j"$JOBS"
  </code>

  <label|remark:To build the test suite configure with DBUILDTESTSON Run it
  with ctest testdir buildqt6 outputonfail {>

  <\remark>
    To build the test suite, configure with <verbatim|-DBUILD_TESTS=ON>. Run
    it with <verbatim|ctest --test-dir build_qt6 --output-on-failure> or the
    corresponding build directory.
  </remark>

  <label|remark:To build the test suite configure with DBUILDTESTSON Run it
  with ctest testdir buildqt6 outputonfail }>

  <label|H2 Assembling the ATHENA tree>

  <subsection|Assembling the <ATHENA> tree>

  The default build stages the private Guile runtime and compiled Scheme
  modules into <verbatim|ATHENA/>. Assemble or refresh the remaining runtime
  files with the outputs from the selected build directory:

  <\code>
    BUILD=build_qt6

    install -m755 "$BUILD/src/ATHENA.bin" ATHENA/bin/

    install -m755 "$BUILD/src/ATHENA-Watchdog" ATHENA/bin/

    install -m755 "$BUILD/src/athena-codex-bridge" ATHENA/bin/

    install -m755 "$BUILD/materials-engine-cargo/release/athena-materials-engine"
    ATHENA/bin/

    install -m755 "$BUILD/tools/athena-transmitter/athena-transmitter"
    ATHENA/bin/

    install -m755 "$BUILD/tools/athena-web-server/athena-web-server"
    ATHENA/bin/

    mkdir -p ATHENA/lib ATHENA/share/ATHENA/web

    cp -a "$BUILD"/x64/lib/libqt6advanceddocking*.so* ATHENA/lib/

    cp -a /path/to/llama.cpp/build/bin/libllama.so* ATHENA/lib/

    cp -a /path/to/llama.cpp/build/bin/libggml*.so* ATHENA/lib/

    cp -a tools/athena-web-server/web/. ATHENA/share/ATHENA/web/
  </code>

  Use <verbatim|BUILD=build_rel> for a RelWithDebInfo tree. For a SYCL
  llama.cpp build, include the matching oneAPI runtime libraries when they
  are not provided by the target system.

  <label|H2 Building RPM, DEB, and AppImage packages>

  <subsection|Building RPM, DEB, and AppImage packages>

  The container builder requires Podman and expects Intel oneAPI under
  <verbatim|/opt/intel/oneapi>. To build the release flavor:

  <\code>
    ATHENA_BUILD_JOBS=16 ATHENA_BUILD_FLAVORS=rel \\

    \ \ tools/container-build/build-athena-container.sh
  </code>

  The principal outputs are:

  <\itemize>
    <item><verbatim|container_build/ATHENA-rel.AppImage>.

    <item><verbatim|container_build/packages/ATHENA-VERSION-rel-linux-x86_64.deb>.

    <item><verbatim|container_build/packages/ATHENA-VERSION-rel-linux-x86_64.rpm>.
  </itemize>

  Set <verbatim|ATHENA_BUILD_FLAVORS> to <verbatim|dev>, <verbatim|rel>, or
  both as required.

  <label|H1 Building ATHENA on other platforms>

  <section|Building <ATHENA> on other platforms>

  <label|H2 Building ATHENA for Windows>

  <subsection|Building <ATHENA> for Windows>

  Windows is not a tested or actively maintained target. A native Windows
  build is theoretically possible with the necessary platform work.

  The repository contains a MinGW-w64 cross-build pipeline under
  <verbatim|tools/windows-deps>. It is currently incomplete because the
  vendored, modified Guile 3.0.10 runtime has not been ported to the Windows
  target. The pipeline must be adapted to build and package that private
  Guile runtime and its BDW-GC dependency before it can produce a current
  <ATHENA> Windows build. The old Guile 1.8 path is obsolete.

  <label|H2 Building ATHENA for macOS, arm64 Linux, and other targets>

  <subsection|Building <ATHENA> for macOS, arm64 Linux, and other targets>

  macOS, arm64 GNU/Linux, and other platforms are theoretically possible with
  appropriate source, dependency, and toolchain modifications. They are not
  currently tested or supported build targets.

  <label|H1 Getting Started with ATHENA>

  <section|Getting Started with <ATHENA>>

  <label|H2 Launching ATHENA>

  <subsection|Launching <ATHENA>>

  If you followed sections 2.1 through 2.4, start <ATHENA> with:

  <\code>
    cd ATHENA

    ./StartATHENA.qt6.wayland.sh
  </code>

  To run through the watchdog:

  <\code>
    ./StartATHENA.qt6.wayland.sh --enable-watchdog
  </code>

  If you followed 2.1 and 2.5, install the RPM or DEB with the package
  manager of the target distribution, or run the AppImage directly.

  <label|remark:X11XCB support is not actively maintained and may contain
  defects Native Wayland is the maintained d {>

  <\remark>
    X11/XCB support is not actively maintained and may contain defects.
    Native Wayland is the maintained desktop path.
  </remark>

  <label|remark:X11XCB support is not actively maintained and may contain
  defects Native Wayland is the maintained d }>

  <label|remark:docking uses xdgtopleveldragv1 KDE Plasma 61 and later
  support the merged protocol in KWin GNOME 48 {>

  <\remark>
    <ATHENA> docking uses <verbatim|xdg-toplevel-drag-v1>. KDE Plasma 6.1 and
    later support the merged protocol in KWin; GNOME 48 and later support it
    in Mutter. Older Wayland compositors may not support detachable-window
    dragging and redocking correctly. The reference system uses Plasma 6.7.4.
  </remark>

  <label|remark:docking uses xdgtopleveldragv1 KDE Plasma 61 and later
  support the merged protocol in KWin GNOME 48 }>

  <label|H2 Setting up ATHENA Transmitter on a server>

  <subsection|Setting up <ATHENA> Transmitter on a server>

  A typical deployment runs a headless <ATHENA> backend on loopback port 8765
  and <verbatim|athena-transmitter> in front of it on port 8766.

  Generate the backend delegation keypair and start the backend:

  <\code>
    ATHENA/bin/ATHENA.bin -H \\

    \ \ --delegation-key-dir /var/lib/athena-backend/delegation \\

    \ \ --generate-server-keypair

    ATHENA/bin/ATHENA.bin -H \\

    \ \ --rag-server /srv/athena/vault \\

    \ \ --rag-listen-address 127.0.0.1 \\

    \ \ --delegation-key-dir /var/lib/athena-backend/delegation
  </code>

  Create <verbatim|/etc/athena-transmitter/config.json>:

  <\code>
    {

    \ \ "listen_address": "127.0.0.1",

    \ \ "listen_port": 8766,

    \ \ "key_dir": "/var/lib/athena-transmitter",

    \ \ "accepted_clients": "/var/lib/athena-transmitter/accepted-clients.json",

    \ \ "pending_clients": "/var/lib/athena-transmitter/pending-clients.json",

    \ \ "upstream": {

    \ \ \ \ "url": "http://127.0.0.1:8765",

    \ \ \ \ "public_key": "BACKEND_PUBLIC_KEY",

    \ \ \ \ "fingerprint": "BACKEND_FINGERPRINT"

    \ \ }

    }
  </code>

  Generate the transmitter keypair:

  <\code>
    ATHENA/bin/athena-transmitter --config
    /etc/athena-transmitter/config.json --generate-keypair
  </code>

  Add trusted client public keys to the transmitter
  <verbatim|accepted-clients.json>:

  <\code>
    {

    \ \ "accepted": [

    \ \ \ \ "CLIENT_PUBLIC_KEY"

    \ \ ]

    }
  </code>

  Add the transmitter public key to the backend
  <verbatim|accepted-clients.json> with role <verbatim|proxy>:

  <\code>
    {

    \ \ "accepted": [

    \ \ \ \ {

    \ \ \ \ \ \ "public_key": "TRANSMITTER_PUBLIC_KEY",

    \ \ \ \ \ \ "role": "proxy"

    \ \ \ \ }

    \ \ ]

    }
  </code>

  Start the transmitter:

  <\code>
    ATHENA/bin/athena-transmitter --config
    /etc/athena-transmitter/config.json
  </code>

  Reference systemd units, the optional XClarity Controller power hook, and a
  Cloudflare Tunnel unit are provided under
  <verbatim|tools/athena-transmitter/deploy>.

  <label|H2 Setting up Web-Accessible ATHENA on a server>

  <subsection|Setting up Web-Accessible <ATHENA> on a server>

  Build the release AppImage as in 2.5, then build the Web-Accessible
  <ATHENA> OCI image:

  <\code>
    tools/athena-web-server/build-image.sh \\

    \ \ --runtime container_build/ATHENA-rel.AppImage \\

    \ \ --image localhost/athena-web:latest
  </code>

  Run the broker as an unprivileged user with rootless Podman and delegated
  cgroup v2 memory and PID controllers. On a systemd host, enable memory
  accounting for the broker user slice before starting it:

  <\code>
    sudo systemctl set-property user-$(id -u).slice MemoryAccounting=yes
  </code>

  A basic loopback broker invocation is:

  <\code>
    ATHENA/bin/athena-web-server \\

    \ \ --listen-address 127.0.0.1 \\

    \ \ --port 8090 \\

    \ \ --image localhost/athena-web:latest \\

    \ \ --max-connections 4 \\

    \ \ --max-memory 4GiB \\

    \ \ --storage-limit 2GiB
  </code>

  Put a WebSocket-capable TLS reverse proxy or tunnel in front of the broker.
  Configure STUN/TURN for the WebRTC media path as required by the
  deployment. The reference production templates under
  <verbatim|tools/athena-web-server/deploy> use a dedicated openSUSE MicroOS
  KVM guest for the broker and rootless session containers, with Cloudflare
  Tunnel for signaling and Cloudflare Realtime TURN for media relay.

  <label|H2 Next steps>

  <subsection|Next steps>

  Start <ATHENA> and read the documentation available from the Help menu. The
  documentation is still under construction. Additional information is
  available at <hlink|athena.evalisk.org|https://athena.evalisk.org/>. For
  questions or build problems, contact <hlink|nuaptan@evalisk.org|mailto:nuaptan@evalisk.org>.
</body>

